WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.243 --> 00:00:07.410
SARA CHOWSKI: Hello, and
welcome to Verily's journey

00:00:07.410 --> 00:00:09.510
to the cloud.

00:00:09.510 --> 00:00:11.593
Today, we'll chat a bit
about Verily, who we are

00:00:11.593 --> 00:00:13.260
and our background,
how we're developing

00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:15.480
in the cloud, my
team, Verily Kumo,

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:17.730
and how we're enabling that
development in partnership

00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:19.230
with Alphabet Cloud.

00:00:19.230 --> 00:00:21.480
Some of the products
we've worked together

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:24.690
to build to enable that
cloud development to be more

00:00:24.690 --> 00:00:27.498
efficient, the adoption
catalysts of those products,

00:00:27.498 --> 00:00:29.790
and then deep dive into one
of the more recent products

00:00:29.790 --> 00:00:32.490
we're excited about, Verily
Cloud, or App Factory.

00:00:32.490 --> 00:00:35.310
And then we'll wrap
it up by chatting

00:00:35.310 --> 00:00:40.080
about where Verily's looking for
the rest of 2020 in the cloud.

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:41.790
But first, we should
do introductions.

00:00:41.790 --> 00:00:43.620
Hello, I'm Sara Chowski.

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:46.290
I work at Verily on
a team called Kumo,

00:00:46.290 --> 00:00:52.440
and I'm joined by Tony Tseng
from Alphabet Cloud in Google.

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:55.740
So Verily-- Verily is Alphabet's
venture in healthcare.

00:00:55.740 --> 00:00:59.970
We're focused on a focused
version of Google's mission,

00:00:59.970 --> 00:01:02.100
to make the world's health
information useful so

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:04.080
that people enjoy
healthier lives.

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:06.510
We sit at the crux of healthcare
research, engineering,

00:01:06.510 --> 00:01:08.400
and data informatics,
and we're focused

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:12.840
on an exponential
improvement of care.

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:15.180
So Verily was founded
as Google Life

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:18.395
Sciences in Google [x] in 2013.

00:01:18.395 --> 00:01:20.520
We've grown since then,
changed our name to Verily,

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:22.880
and we now have
over 1,000 employees

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:25.170
in Verily and joint ventures.

00:01:25.170 --> 00:01:30.090
We work with 32 top-tier pharma
and medical device companies.

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:32.190
And we partner in
three different spaces.

00:01:32.190 --> 00:01:33.990
We partner across
care solutions,

00:01:33.990 --> 00:01:36.690
where we work with health
systems and employers

00:01:36.690 --> 00:01:39.120
to develop tools and
interventions optimizing

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:40.410
care delivery.

00:01:40.410 --> 00:01:42.960
We work in research solutions,
creating an ecosystem

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:46.110
of sponsors and health systems
to accelerate studies and make

00:01:46.110 --> 00:01:48.130
it easier to generate evidence.

00:01:48.130 --> 00:01:50.730
And finally, we collaborate
in innovation solutions,

00:01:50.730 --> 00:01:54.780
working together on next-gen
devices and connected solutions

00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:57.840
that empower research
and care outside the four

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:01.260
walls of a clinic.

00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:04.600
So one product Verily works
on is Project Baseline.

00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:07.953
Project Baseline collects and
organizes terabytes of data

00:02:07.953 --> 00:02:09.370
and is the future
of understanding

00:02:09.370 --> 00:02:10.810
comprehensive health.

00:02:10.810 --> 00:02:12.700
We collect data from
traditional sources,

00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:15.183
like clinic healthcare
records, as well as

00:02:15.183 --> 00:02:16.600
from more
non-traditional sources,

00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:20.080
like data collected at home
about behavior and medication

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:23.110
and remote wearable
devices and location.

00:02:23.110 --> 00:02:27.270
We pool together this data
and organize and aggregate it.

00:02:27.270 --> 00:02:29.825
And using the Project Baseline
ecosystem we've built,

00:02:29.825 --> 00:02:31.200
we improved the
speed and quality

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:34.950
of evidence generation of other
companies in pharma, device,

00:02:34.950 --> 00:02:38.530
and health systems so that they
can focus on what they do best.

00:02:38.530 --> 00:02:40.980
We sit at the center to
connect participants to studies

00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:43.200
and develop technology
to enable studies

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:45.200
to move more efficiently.

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:48.030
And as you can imagine, these
partners and participants

00:02:48.030 --> 00:02:50.140
have varying needs and
different infrastructure,

00:02:50.140 --> 00:02:52.140
and we want to meet them
where they are and kind

00:02:52.140 --> 00:02:55.320
of collaborate in a public
cloud, which has led us

00:02:55.320 --> 00:02:57.510
to developing in the cloud.

00:02:57.510 --> 00:02:59.940
As I said, we gain
several benefits

00:02:59.940 --> 00:03:02.520
from the cloud, one of which
is co-development and data

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:03.910
sharing with partners.

00:03:03.910 --> 00:03:08.220
We also get fast development
and prototyping from the cloud.

00:03:08.220 --> 00:03:11.490
We reduce new hire ramp up time,
because all of the tool chains

00:03:11.490 --> 00:03:13.740
are tool chains that new
hires are more familiar with,

00:03:13.740 --> 00:03:15.000
as they're public.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:18.720
And finally, we help Google
dogfood its GCP cloud offering.

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:20.790
Dogfood is a
tradition at Alphabet

00:03:20.790 --> 00:03:24.450
where we kind of preview and
work with products before they

00:03:24.450 --> 00:03:28.140
hit market and kind of
provide feedback and help

00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:30.820
fine-tune these products.

00:03:30.820 --> 00:03:32.830
So Verily's GCP history--

00:03:32.830 --> 00:03:35.800
as you can see, our use
of GCP has just gone

00:03:35.800 --> 00:03:37.330
up and to the right.

00:03:37.330 --> 00:03:39.915
We've been adopting GCP
over the course of years,

00:03:39.915 --> 00:03:42.040
and we've hit a lot of the
same cloud adoption pain

00:03:42.040 --> 00:03:44.920
points that all
GCP customers hit.

00:03:44.920 --> 00:03:48.010
A sample of these
hard challenges

00:03:48.010 --> 00:03:51.220
include management of
external partners' access

00:03:51.220 --> 00:03:54.280
to data, making sure that
if there are agreements

00:03:54.280 --> 00:03:56.650
to acknowledge that these
partners do so before seeing

00:03:56.650 --> 00:04:01.510
data, that their membership
would expire in a timely manner

00:04:01.510 --> 00:04:03.760
and possibly be refreshed.

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:07.330
We worked to audit cloud
resources and their access,

00:04:07.330 --> 00:04:09.970
make sure our IAM policies
are exactly what we want them

00:04:09.970 --> 00:04:14.050
to be, and that we understand
who is accessing our data

00:04:14.050 --> 00:04:17.620
and have technical
leads just kind of check

00:04:17.620 --> 00:04:20.649
on and audit these accesses
and resources configuration

00:04:20.649 --> 00:04:22.089
from time to time.

00:04:22.089 --> 00:04:25.390
And finally, just streamline
of creation and management

00:04:25.390 --> 00:04:27.122
of apps and resources.

00:04:27.122 --> 00:04:29.080
And as we kind of hit
some of these roadblocks,

00:04:29.080 --> 00:04:31.413
they've pushed us to create
new pieces of infrastructure

00:04:31.413 --> 00:04:33.670
to solve these common
pain points together

00:04:33.670 --> 00:04:36.628
with Verily and Alphabet.

00:04:36.628 --> 00:04:38.920
And Alphabet Cloud is in the
process of productionizing

00:04:38.920 --> 00:04:42.170
and releasing these products.

00:04:42.170 --> 00:04:44.800
So my team, Verily Kumo, is
one of the central cloud-native

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:46.600
teams at Verily.

00:04:46.600 --> 00:04:48.760
I learned, possibly too
many months into my tenure

00:04:48.760 --> 00:04:51.310
on Kumo, that Kumo means
"cloud" in Japanese, which

00:04:51.310 --> 00:04:53.270
is quite fitting for our team.

00:04:53.270 --> 00:04:55.720
Our mission is to make
Verily's use of the cloud

00:04:55.720 --> 00:04:59.050
safer, simpler, and
more secure by default.

00:04:59.050 --> 00:05:03.280
We work with teams in Verily
and understand their use cases

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:05.770
and iterate on their
cloud resource management.

00:05:05.770 --> 00:05:07.780
We collaborate
with Verily DevOps

00:05:07.780 --> 00:05:09.760
to promote reliable and
sustainable software

00:05:09.760 --> 00:05:11.325
development practices.

00:05:11.325 --> 00:05:15.670
Kumo manages IAM best practices
and enforces Org policies

00:05:15.670 --> 00:05:18.003
on our GCP resources,
to kind of make sure

00:05:18.003 --> 00:05:19.420
that those resources
are following

00:05:19.420 --> 00:05:21.160
the guidelines we need them to.

00:05:21.160 --> 00:05:23.985
And finally, we helped develop
custom Terraform modules.

00:05:23.985 --> 00:05:25.610
And these are fairly
specific Terraform

00:05:25.610 --> 00:05:28.990
modules that enforce best
practices, consistency,

00:05:28.990 --> 00:05:30.112
and security.

00:05:30.112 --> 00:05:32.320
And they're used throughout
the company both directly

00:05:32.320 --> 00:05:35.520
and, as you'll see later,
in App Factory as well.

00:05:35.520 --> 00:05:37.810
And as I said, my
team collaborates

00:05:37.810 --> 00:05:39.530
with Alphabet Cloud
to do all of this,

00:05:39.530 --> 00:05:43.982
so I'll hand it over to Tony
to talk a bit about his team.

00:05:43.982 --> 00:05:45.190
TONY TSENG: Thank you, Sarah.

00:05:45.190 --> 00:05:46.010
Hi, everyone.

00:05:46.010 --> 00:05:46.730
I'm Tony.

00:05:46.730 --> 00:05:50.050
I'm one of the tech leads in the
Alphabet Cloud team at Google.

00:05:50.050 --> 00:05:53.020
So our team was formed about
two and a half years ago,

00:05:53.020 --> 00:05:56.650
and our team's mission
is to enable GCP adoption

00:05:56.650 --> 00:05:59.470
across Alphabet companies
so that ultimately, we

00:05:59.470 --> 00:06:03.560
can improve our platform and
the overall customer experience.

00:06:03.560 --> 00:06:06.560
In other words, we're
here to reduce friction.

00:06:06.560 --> 00:06:08.440
So in the past two
and a half years,

00:06:08.440 --> 00:06:11.720
we have been using GCP like
an external customer would,

00:06:11.720 --> 00:06:14.440
and we've seen our share
of friction points.

00:06:14.440 --> 00:06:17.260
I'm going to quickly go
over three examples here,

00:06:17.260 --> 00:06:20.320
and I suspect some of you might
have seen them before too.

00:06:20.320 --> 00:06:24.260
The first example is Istio
to MySQL connectivity.

00:06:24.260 --> 00:06:28.780
So that mTLS in this PERMISSIVE
mode does not work with MySQL.

00:06:28.780 --> 00:06:32.360
You'll either need to change
it to strict or disable mTLS

00:06:32.360 --> 00:06:33.760
altogether.

00:06:33.760 --> 00:06:37.570
This is a well-documented issue
by now, but once in a while,

00:06:37.570 --> 00:06:39.640
new people will still hit it.

00:06:39.640 --> 00:06:44.810
The second example is automatic
HTTP to HTTPS redirect.

00:06:44.810 --> 00:06:47.740
So earlier this year,
Google Cloud Load Balancer

00:06:47.740 --> 00:06:48.960
rolled out this feature.

00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:51.190
But before it was
available, we had

00:06:51.190 --> 00:06:53.350
to add another layer
of load balancer

00:06:53.350 --> 00:06:55.720
in order to do this redirect.

00:06:55.720 --> 00:06:59.460
The third example is
about GCP API enablement.

00:06:59.460 --> 00:07:02.440
So in our Terraform modules,
we need to conditionally

00:07:02.440 --> 00:07:05.500
wait for an API to
be enabled before we

00:07:05.500 --> 00:07:07.510
can do any kind of operation.

00:07:07.510 --> 00:07:10.240
Otherwise, it's going
to result in an error.

00:07:10.240 --> 00:07:12.250
So for some of these
friction points,

00:07:12.250 --> 00:07:15.850
we'll try to add internal
priority so that the fixes can

00:07:15.850 --> 00:07:17.320
be rolled out sooner.

00:07:17.320 --> 00:07:20.620
And for others, we've created
workarounds and custom

00:07:20.620 --> 00:07:21.460
solutions.

00:07:23.970 --> 00:07:26.300
So as the next step
of custom solutions,

00:07:26.300 --> 00:07:29.840
we have launched three internal
products within Verily.

00:07:29.840 --> 00:07:32.460
The first product is
called Verily Groups.

00:07:32.460 --> 00:07:34.700
Verily Groups is an
application layer

00:07:34.700 --> 00:07:36.540
built on top of Google Groups.

00:07:36.540 --> 00:07:38.730
It has two additional features.

00:07:38.730 --> 00:07:41.600
The first one is
membership expiration.

00:07:41.600 --> 00:07:44.690
Verily does not wish groups
to have permanent members.

00:07:44.690 --> 00:07:48.060
Therefore, all members will
have an expiration period.

00:07:48.060 --> 00:07:51.110
And also, when the
expiration time approaches,

00:07:51.110 --> 00:07:53.480
notifications will be
sent out to both the group

00:07:53.480 --> 00:07:55.680
admin and the group member.

00:07:55.680 --> 00:07:59.240
The second key feature
is about data agreements.

00:07:59.240 --> 00:08:03.140
So some of these groups are used
for access control purposes.

00:08:03.140 --> 00:08:05.930
So before a member can
gain access to a group,

00:08:05.930 --> 00:08:08.770
they have to acknowledge
some usage agreements.

00:08:08.770 --> 00:08:11.150
And when an acknowledgment
has been made,

00:08:11.150 --> 00:08:14.860
it will have to be
renewed periodically.

00:08:14.860 --> 00:08:17.200
The second product
is called Aditum.

00:08:17.200 --> 00:08:20.440
So Aditum is a service that
offers security, audit,

00:08:20.440 --> 00:08:23.080
and cost control
governance for Verily.

00:08:23.080 --> 00:08:26.730
It ingests GCP resource
metadata, audit logs,

00:08:26.730 --> 00:08:29.200
and IAM policies in
order to generate

00:08:29.200 --> 00:08:34.700
periodic reviews of reports
for Verily leads to review.

00:08:34.700 --> 00:08:37.390
It also has integration
with Config Validator

00:08:37.390 --> 00:08:40.720
so that it can scan for
anomalies and violations

00:08:40.720 --> 00:08:43.700
in GCP resource configuration.

00:08:43.700 --> 00:08:46.810
And finally, the third product
is called Verily Cloud.

00:08:46.810 --> 00:08:49.660
Verily Cloud is a
Kubernetes-based application

00:08:49.660 --> 00:08:52.540
platform created by
us in collaboration

00:08:52.540 --> 00:08:54.010
with Verily teams.

00:08:54.010 --> 00:08:55.900
We like it a lot
so far, and we're

00:08:55.900 --> 00:08:58.180
planning to make it
available externally

00:08:58.180 --> 00:09:00.220
under the name of App Factory.

00:09:00.220 --> 00:09:02.530
So for the rest of
this presentation,

00:09:02.530 --> 00:09:05.500
you might hear the names
Verily Cloud and App Factory

00:09:05.500 --> 00:09:09.550
being used interchangeably.

00:09:09.550 --> 00:09:12.860
The initial motivation for
us to create App Factory

00:09:12.860 --> 00:09:15.450
is to reduce the
time to service.

00:09:15.450 --> 00:09:18.420
The one common complaint that
we keep hearing over and over

00:09:18.420 --> 00:09:21.270
again is that it takes
too long for a new team

00:09:21.270 --> 00:09:23.570
to get started on cloud.

00:09:23.570 --> 00:09:25.620
Oftentimes, a
developer will spend

00:09:25.620 --> 00:09:28.110
a lot of time learning
about GCP configuration

00:09:28.110 --> 00:09:31.380
details and their internal
security standards.

00:09:31.380 --> 00:09:33.870
Now both of these
are really important,

00:09:33.870 --> 00:09:35.970
but they don't
meaningfully contribute

00:09:35.970 --> 00:09:38.970
to the quality of software
developer deployment.

00:09:38.970 --> 00:09:42.070
They also don't change very
much from team to team.

00:09:42.070 --> 00:09:45.300
Therefore, our hypothesis
is that by offering

00:09:45.300 --> 00:09:48.810
an opinionated platform
with automatic guardrails,

00:09:48.810 --> 00:09:51.030
we can significantly
reduce this ramp-up time.

00:09:54.980 --> 00:09:58.170
There are four key
features of App Factory.

00:09:58.170 --> 00:10:01.430
The first one is about cloud
resource configurations.

00:10:01.430 --> 00:10:03.380
App Factory enables
you to define

00:10:03.380 --> 00:10:06.440
custom abstractions of
cloud resources backed

00:10:06.440 --> 00:10:08.540
by Terraform modules.

00:10:08.540 --> 00:10:12.140
To use an analogy, instead
of a hardware store that

00:10:12.140 --> 00:10:14.540
sells nuts and
bolts, App Factory

00:10:14.540 --> 00:10:17.660
should be more like a furniture
store that sells fully

00:10:17.660 --> 00:10:20.420
assembled desks and chairs.

00:10:20.420 --> 00:10:23.750
In this basic usage, you can
have a one-to-one mapping

00:10:23.750 --> 00:10:27.260
between App Factory resources
and cloud resources.

00:10:27.260 --> 00:10:28.910
The only difference
there is that you

00:10:28.910 --> 00:10:32.000
may take away a lot of
settings and replace them

00:10:32.000 --> 00:10:34.370
with fixed values
that are appropriate

00:10:34.370 --> 00:10:37.230
for your specific application.

00:10:37.230 --> 00:10:40.730
But for most applications,
they need more than just

00:10:40.730 --> 00:10:43.340
a cost-equal instance
or just a GCS

00:10:43.340 --> 00:10:46.400
bucket or just Pub/Sub topic.

00:10:46.400 --> 00:10:49.610
They actually need a suite
of different GCP resources

00:10:49.610 --> 00:10:52.850
to work together
as a cohesive unit.

00:10:52.850 --> 00:10:55.430
So App Factory enables
you to define this higher

00:10:55.430 --> 00:10:57.390
level of abstraction.

00:10:57.390 --> 00:10:59.300
So their developers
will only need

00:10:59.300 --> 00:11:02.510
to deal with a single
logical entity, as opposed

00:11:02.510 --> 00:11:07.740
to five or six or 10
different cloud resources.

00:11:07.740 --> 00:11:11.570
The second key feature is about
rapid application development.

00:11:11.570 --> 00:11:14.540
As a developer, I want to get
to the business logic part

00:11:14.540 --> 00:11:16.470
as fast as possible.

00:11:16.470 --> 00:11:20.840
I'm not very interested in the
boilerplate or bootstrap code.

00:11:20.840 --> 00:11:23.450
App Factory enables you
to bring your own set

00:11:23.450 --> 00:11:26.900
of service templates for
your developers to build on.

00:11:26.900 --> 00:11:30.200
It is much easier to start
from a working example

00:11:30.200 --> 00:11:33.270
than to start from scratch.

00:11:33.270 --> 00:11:35.790
The third feature is
about the integration

00:11:35.790 --> 00:11:37.710
with Git repositories.

00:11:37.710 --> 00:11:41.600
So all the configurations are
stored in a Git repository.

00:11:41.600 --> 00:11:44.010
So with a version
control system,

00:11:44.010 --> 00:11:46.660
we get a few benefits for free.

00:11:46.660 --> 00:11:47.910
We have code reviews.

00:11:47.910 --> 00:11:49.290
We have audit logs.

00:11:49.290 --> 00:11:51.460
We have the ability
to roll back.

00:11:51.460 --> 00:11:55.120
We can also plug in
presubmit checks.

00:11:55.120 --> 00:11:57.880
And finally, App
Factory is a bridge

00:11:57.880 --> 00:12:00.430
between click-ops and Git-ops.

00:12:00.430 --> 00:12:04.510
We recognize that not
everybody is a YAML enthusiast.

00:12:04.510 --> 00:12:08.560
Somebody, or some of us, still
prefer to work with WebUI.

00:12:08.560 --> 00:12:11.410
So App Factory can take
the input from WebUI

00:12:11.410 --> 00:12:13.850
and generate a
corresponding pull request.

00:12:13.850 --> 00:12:16.780
And also depending on
your specific setup,

00:12:16.780 --> 00:12:19.000
you can choose to
skip the review part

00:12:19.000 --> 00:12:22.090
and commit directly
to the repository.

00:12:22.090 --> 00:12:25.600
And next, we're going to
show a demo of App Factory.

00:12:25.600 --> 00:12:30.550
And after the demo, Sara
will go over the road map.

00:12:30.550 --> 00:12:33.070
This is a demo for Verily Cloud.

00:12:33.070 --> 00:12:35.800
Verily Cloud is an internal
application platform

00:12:35.800 --> 00:12:37.330
built on Kubernetes.

00:12:37.330 --> 00:12:39.920
It's proven to be
quite useful so far,

00:12:39.920 --> 00:12:44.200
and we are exploring its utility
as an external product offering

00:12:44.200 --> 00:12:46.390
under the name App Factory.

00:12:46.390 --> 00:12:48.490
In this demo, we're going
to show the workflow

00:12:48.490 --> 00:12:51.130
to create a cloud
environment on GCP

00:12:51.130 --> 00:12:54.010
and to deploy a
simple application.

00:12:54.010 --> 00:12:56.590
The first step is
to create a product.

00:12:56.590 --> 00:12:58.930
A product on Verily
Cloud roughly maps

00:12:58.930 --> 00:13:02.350
to a set of Git repositories,
cloud environments,

00:13:02.350 --> 00:13:04.300
and software services.

00:13:04.300 --> 00:13:07.180
In terms of the GCP
resource hierarchy,

00:13:07.180 --> 00:13:09.730
you can think of a
product as a folder,

00:13:09.730 --> 00:13:13.030
and there is one GCP
project per environment

00:13:13.030 --> 00:13:15.160
underneath that
folder to contain

00:13:15.160 --> 00:13:18.040
the environment-specific
resources.

00:13:18.040 --> 00:13:21.430
Now let's go to the
product creation screen.

00:13:21.430 --> 00:13:23.650
Other than the name
and description,

00:13:23.650 --> 00:13:27.520
you will see that there are also
the IAM groups and Break-Glass

00:13:27.520 --> 00:13:28.360
groups.

00:13:28.360 --> 00:13:32.710
The IAM groups have read-only
permissions on prod and preprod

00:13:32.710 --> 00:13:33.970
environments.

00:13:33.970 --> 00:13:37.000
The Break-Glass groups
have full access.

00:13:37.000 --> 00:13:39.310
Break-Glass comes from
the phrase "in case

00:13:39.310 --> 00:13:41.620
of emergency, break glass."

00:13:41.620 --> 00:13:43.880
Access to the group
is based on demand

00:13:43.880 --> 00:13:46.090
and should only be done
when there is a need

00:13:46.090 --> 00:13:48.100
to make emergency changes.

00:13:48.100 --> 00:13:51.300
And the membership has
a short expiration time.

00:13:51.300 --> 00:13:54.670
At the bottom here, you'll
see that Verily Cloud creates

00:13:54.670 --> 00:13:57.220
five environments
by default. That's

00:13:57.220 --> 00:13:59.910
based on Verily's standard
engineering practice,

00:13:59.910 --> 00:14:03.350
and it is completely
customizable.

00:14:03.350 --> 00:14:05.973
Now I'm going to enter
the product parameters

00:14:05.973 --> 00:14:07.265
and start the creation process.

00:14:20.180 --> 00:14:22.192
Going back to the
product list screen,

00:14:22.192 --> 00:14:23.650
you can see that
the product is now

00:14:23.650 --> 00:14:25.660
in the pending creation state.

00:14:25.660 --> 00:14:27.770
Here is where Verily
Cloud makes the connection

00:14:27.770 --> 00:14:29.920
between click-ops and Git-ops.

00:14:29.920 --> 00:14:32.810
Verily Cloud has generated
a GitHub pull request

00:14:32.810 --> 00:14:36.010
based on the product parameters
that we have specified earlier.

00:14:36.010 --> 00:14:38.900
It's showing "Pending Creation"
because the pull request

00:14:38.900 --> 00:14:40.260
has not been merged yet.

00:14:40.260 --> 00:14:41.510
Let's review the pull request.

00:14:47.430 --> 00:14:50.700
This pull request contains
multiple files and directories.

00:14:50.700 --> 00:14:53.280
There is one directory
per environment.

00:14:53.280 --> 00:14:55.320
And if we scroll
all the way down,

00:14:55.320 --> 00:14:58.650
we'll see a product, that yaml
file, that contains the product

00:14:58.650 --> 00:14:59.940
specification.

00:14:59.940 --> 00:15:02.550
I'd like to call your
attention to these two fields.

00:15:02.550 --> 00:15:06.180
Every product has a config
repo and a deployment repo.

00:15:06.180 --> 00:15:08.370
Config repo is the
repository that we're

00:15:08.370 --> 00:15:09.870
looking at right now.

00:15:09.870 --> 00:15:12.960
It is meant to contain
human-readable configurations

00:15:12.960 --> 00:15:14.940
at the right level
of abstraction

00:15:14.940 --> 00:15:17.670
to be defined by your
infrastructure team.

00:15:17.670 --> 00:15:21.390
The deployment repo contains the
fully expanded configurations

00:15:21.390 --> 00:15:24.390
that can be directly applied
to the actuation layer.

00:15:24.390 --> 00:15:27.330
In other words, it is
a two-step process.

00:15:27.330 --> 00:15:30.030
Human-readable changes
in the config repo

00:15:30.030 --> 00:15:32.670
will be translated into
machine-readable changes

00:15:32.670 --> 00:15:35.820
in the deployment repo,
and then it'll be actuated.

00:15:35.820 --> 00:15:38.775
Now I'm going to review and
approve and merge the request.

00:15:42.790 --> 00:15:45.410
It will take about a minute to
complete the entire process.

00:15:52.440 --> 00:15:54.580
After the product's
been created,

00:15:54.580 --> 00:15:56.780
let's go to the product string.

00:15:56.780 --> 00:15:59.900
In the left hand here, there is
a list of all the environments

00:15:59.900 --> 00:16:02.630
associated with this product.

00:16:02.630 --> 00:16:05.840
So right now, there's nothing
in my dev environment.

00:16:05.840 --> 00:16:08.630
As my next step, I'm going
to create a new GKE cluster.

00:16:12.860 --> 00:16:15.370
So other than the machine
type and node count,

00:16:15.370 --> 00:16:17.230
there is nothing else
for me to change.

00:16:17.230 --> 00:16:19.645
That's because the Verily
team has already pre-defined

00:16:19.645 --> 00:16:22.120
all the rest of the parameters.

00:16:22.120 --> 00:16:24.190
And after I hit
the Submit button,

00:16:24.190 --> 00:16:27.010
it's going to trigger the
creation of another pull

00:16:27.010 --> 00:16:28.730
request.

00:16:28.730 --> 00:16:29.880
And then we can review it.

00:16:33.310 --> 00:16:35.510
So the first thing here
on the pull request

00:16:35.510 --> 00:16:40.140
is a Terraform plan output
that shows the expected changes

00:16:40.140 --> 00:16:44.820
on the GCP side once this
pull request is merged.

00:16:44.820 --> 00:16:48.930
If I scroll down a little bit,
other than the GKE cluster

00:16:48.930 --> 00:16:52.980
configuration, there is
also the configuration

00:16:52.980 --> 00:16:54.780
for the alert policies.

00:16:54.780 --> 00:16:56.760
Again, I did not define these.

00:16:56.760 --> 00:17:01.710
They have been created
already by the Verily teams.

00:17:01.710 --> 00:17:03.960
And let's take a look
at the yaml file.

00:17:03.960 --> 00:17:05.560
The email file is pretty simple.

00:17:05.560 --> 00:17:07.800
It only contains the
parameters that I just

00:17:07.800 --> 00:17:09.839
entered in the WebUI.

00:17:09.839 --> 00:17:20.490
So I'm going to approve this
change and merge this request,

00:17:20.490 --> 00:17:25.230
and the GKE clusters should
be ready in a few minutes.

00:17:25.230 --> 00:17:28.020
My GKE cluster is
ready to serve now.

00:17:28.020 --> 00:17:30.960
And I'm going to
add a service next.

00:17:30.960 --> 00:17:33.690
At this point, I should
mention that both the resource

00:17:33.690 --> 00:17:36.990
and service templates are
meant to be customized.

00:17:36.990 --> 00:17:39.630
They should be tailor-made
to fit the exact needs

00:17:39.630 --> 00:17:41.730
of your application team.

00:17:41.730 --> 00:17:45.060
And here, I'm adding a
JAVA-based gRPC service.

00:17:50.080 --> 00:17:52.930
And because I'm deploying
this my dev environment,

00:17:52.930 --> 00:17:55.630
I'm going to specify that
a new rollout will be

00:17:55.630 --> 00:17:57.320
created on every single commit.

00:18:02.660 --> 00:18:06.650
And now a pull request should
be generated, and let's go ahead

00:18:06.650 --> 00:18:07.430
and review it.

00:18:14.330 --> 00:18:19.000
The interesting bit here is
that there is a repoURL field.

00:18:19.000 --> 00:18:21.510
After this pull
request is merged,

00:18:21.510 --> 00:18:25.090
it's going to create a new
repository of this location,

00:18:25.090 --> 00:18:26.980
and it's going to
contain this simple code.

00:18:39.960 --> 00:18:42.090
The service creation
has finished.

00:18:42.090 --> 00:18:42.870
Let's take a look.

00:18:45.960 --> 00:18:49.470
And this URL points to
the source repository that

00:18:49.470 --> 00:18:52.010
contains the generated code.

00:18:52.010 --> 00:18:55.220
And here, we can see that there
are a few build-related files.

00:18:55.220 --> 00:18:58.540
And if we look inside
the source directory,

00:18:58.540 --> 00:19:02.160
we'll see that we have the
protocol buffer definition, as

00:19:02.160 --> 00:19:04.960
well as a Java skeleton code.

00:19:04.960 --> 00:19:08.310
And because we specified
to rollout on every commit,

00:19:08.310 --> 00:19:11.190
this initial commit should
generate a deployment

00:19:11.190 --> 00:19:12.135
on my GKE cluster.

00:19:15.300 --> 00:19:19.700
And here, let's take a
look at the Workloads page.

00:19:19.700 --> 00:19:22.560
And the deployments are
already up and running.

00:19:22.560 --> 00:19:24.240
And this concludes our demo.

00:19:24.240 --> 00:19:26.350
Thank you.

00:19:26.350 --> 00:19:29.490
SARA CHOWSKI: Welcome back,
and thanks for the demo, Tony.

00:19:29.490 --> 00:19:31.530
As we look forward
to the rest of 2020

00:19:31.530 --> 00:19:35.190
and Verily in the cloud, we
have a few projects in mind.

00:19:35.190 --> 00:19:37.740
First, we want to work
towards enabling prototyping

00:19:37.740 --> 00:19:39.600
playgrounds for developers.

00:19:39.600 --> 00:19:42.030
As I said previously,
we've enabled

00:19:42.030 --> 00:19:44.590
a lot of IAM best
practices and GCP Org

00:19:44.590 --> 00:19:47.400
policies in our
organization hierarchy

00:19:47.400 --> 00:19:49.710
to kind of keep data safe
and secure by default.

00:19:49.710 --> 00:19:53.940
But this slows down the
great development velocity

00:19:53.940 --> 00:19:56.310
we get when we use the cloud.

00:19:56.310 --> 00:20:01.140
And so we want to be able to
enable playground life cycles

00:20:01.140 --> 00:20:04.202
and make sure that developers
can easily deploy a project

00:20:04.202 --> 00:20:06.660
and try some things without
going through a large exemption

00:20:06.660 --> 00:20:07.853
process.

00:20:07.853 --> 00:20:09.270
Looking towards
App Factory, we're

00:20:09.270 --> 00:20:11.760
working to make templated
generic applications,

00:20:11.760 --> 00:20:15.240
independent of
language or framework,

00:20:15.240 --> 00:20:16.950
be able to be deployed.

00:20:16.950 --> 00:20:19.290
We want to focus on
test infrastructure,

00:20:19.290 --> 00:20:23.550
adding integration and load
testing that are easy to setup.

00:20:23.550 --> 00:20:26.610
We want to work to support
more cloud resources.

00:20:26.610 --> 00:20:30.330
And this is just increasing
the breadth of GCP resources

00:20:30.330 --> 00:20:33.040
we have, as well as looking
potentially to multi-cloud,

00:20:33.040 --> 00:20:35.940
being able to flex to
where our partners are.

00:20:35.940 --> 00:20:38.370
We want to be able to customize
release management-- name

00:20:38.370 --> 00:20:41.610
custom environments, add
additional environments,

00:20:41.610 --> 00:20:46.180
and change release pipelines
to suit developer needs.

00:20:46.180 --> 00:20:50.040
And finally, more generic
automatic monitoring setup,

00:20:50.040 --> 00:20:51.600
so that when people
set up a project,

00:20:51.600 --> 00:20:54.863
it just has that monitoring and
telemetry available for them

00:20:54.863 --> 00:20:55.530
from the get-go.

00:20:58.070 --> 00:20:59.990
And so that's what we
have for you today.

00:20:59.990 --> 00:21:01.448
If you're interested
in anything we

00:21:01.448 --> 00:21:04.240
talked about or using the tools
that Googlers and Alphabet use

00:21:04.240 --> 00:21:06.912
to speed up their public
cloud adoption efforts,

00:21:06.912 --> 00:21:09.370
please reach out to Doug Platts,
the Alphabet Cloud product

00:21:09.370 --> 00:21:11.040
manager.

00:21:11.040 --> 00:21:13.800
And thank you so much
for joining us today.

